			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468696">
									

<p>So I want to like this, because it's very well-written&#8212;and I don't say that lightly, some of the character writing is so solid that I could easily picture the scenes happening in my head as I read them, which is something I struggle with.</p>
<p>What is holding me back from giving this a +1 is two points, one major and one minor.</p>
<p>The minor is that it doesn't seem to fit the contest theme that well, which, well, is not that big of a deal in my opinion.</p>
<p>More glaring is that you seem to have kind of committed an X-Men here, I'm not sure how I feel about the (real) loss of native land to outsiders being conflated with the (fictional) oppression of anomalous persons. Intentionally or not, that is essentially what this piece does. I do not know how you'd fix that barring a total rewrite (which would be, you know, a lot to ask).</p>
<p>The bits where Hotah speaks on Henry's virtue in particular feel kind of off to me in a way I find it difficult to articulate. From the perspective of the narrative, it's hard to say he's wrong, but taking it Doylistically, combined with the issue I mentioned above, it strikes a weird tone.</p>
<p>I'm not going to vote on this for the time being. I need to think on it, I think.</p>

							</div>

								
