<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Summary:&nbsp;This&nbsp;article&nbsp;was&nbsp;really&nbsp;good&nbsp;in&nbsp;most&nbsp;ways,&nbsp;bad&nbsp;at&nbsp;at&nbsp;twoish&nbsp;things,&nbsp;and&nbsp;I&nbsp;don't&nbsp;like&nbsp;the&nbsp;idea&nbsp;of&nbsp;it&nbsp;as&nbsp;SCP-5000</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Explanation.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This is a very good article for plenty of reasons. It has good characters, a good narrative for the most part, it ties in with other (also very good) SCPs in logical, interesting, engaging ways that don't totally leave you feeling like an idiot if you don't know the referenced SCPs. It's been said that this is too long for its own good, but I disagree. Its length is used appropriately, and most of the elements fit very well together. Hell, it even does the theme of a mystery well enough. Like, it isn't a massive mystery in your face, but it's enough of one to qualify for the contest. After all, the theme is supposed to help inspire, not pointlessly limit.</p>
<p>However, and for other reasons, I have to say that I don't like the idea of this as the SCP-5000. The thousands are good ways to signal the progress of the foundation as a living work of fiction, and I feel that this doesn't do a great job of that. It uses the idea of &quot;foundation as the bad guys&quot; in a way that really isn't great? I've always been sort of annoyed by that trope since every time I see it it feels kinda samey, and ya know, the whole X-Men thing.</p>
<p>If this article becomes SCP-5000, I worry that it could put the trope of &quot;the foundation as the bad guys&quot; on a pedestal and set a precedent, and inspire even more people to use it, which I fear would kind of suck. The possibility of a series VI with &quot;the foundation kinda being sucky&quot; as a common theme makes me shudder</p>
<p>Overall, it is a very good article with a questionable ending that uses a trope I don't like in a way I don't like. I'll no-vote for now, but after the contest ends, I'd be happy to drop by and give it a +1.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
After reading that, here's a little extra from me.
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">spoilers!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">spoiiiiilers!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>If I was going to make any changes, I would have it end with a reveal that Eric's power was more general mind manipulation, and that Graham wasn't just having an &quot;OMG the foundation is bad?&quot; moment. Graham would at first do the same things, up until the end, when they'd refuse to let him come with them. They leave without him, Graham realizes he's been manipulated,&quot;Wait, why did I think the foundation was bad?&quot; and we have a little more info. It preserves the idea that they just wanna be left alone (so says I) and ticks a happy part of your brain by subverting a generally annoying trope in a way that makes you go &quot;ooooh cool.&quot; Anyways, that's my take.</p>
</div>
</div>
