			<div class="content" id="post-content-4471302">
									

<p>The interviews killed this for me, honestly. I understand wanting to get the story across, but they drag on for <em>quite</em> some time. This wouldn't be terrible if the characters felt human or likable enough, but they all sounded the same. There wasn't truly any personality or identity given to them &#8212; they're just rattling off blocks of exposition at a time &#8212; with a small, occasional bit of emoting.</p>
<p>(This mostly holds true for the first interview, and some of the second, but I did not read past that point.)</p>
<p>If it's absolutely inherent to go over so much content in detailing background, I think this would need to offload some of the exposition into either some form of recovered materials (diaries, historical records) so more focus could be given to letting characters reveal their nature instead of reciting the story.</p>

